    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Related Rates</title>

            <p workspace="1in">
                Today is group presentation day! Each group (or at least someone from each group) will come up to present their problem. The worksheet, where you are turning in all of Part 1, and the particular problem your group did from Part 2, is due on Wednesday.
            </p>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Linear Approximation and Differentials</title>
            
            <p component="b" workspace="4in">
                A <em>differential</em> is a small change in a variable. So for <m>y = f(x)</m>, the differential <m>dx</m> is a small change in <m>x</m>. With this, we can define
                <md>
                    dy = f'(x) \ dx
                </md> which should be a small change in <m>y</m>. How can we see that?
                
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                A <em>differential</em> is a small change in a variable. So for <m>y = f(x)</m>, the differential <m>dx</m> is a small change in <m>x</m>. With this, we can define
                <md>
                    dy = f'(x) \ dx
                </md> which should be a small change in <m>y</m>. How can we see that? dx is <m>\Delta x</m> and pull from linear approximation.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Assume that a sphere is made of radius <quantity><mag>5</mag><unit base="inch"/></quantity> with error of <quantity><mag>0.3</mag><unit base="inch"/></quantity>. What is the expected error in the volume of the sphere?
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>L'Hospital's Rule</title>

            <p>
                For one of our first applications of the derivative, we will return to limits.
            </p>

            <example workspace="2in">
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Evaluate each of the following limits:
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow 4} \frac{\sqrt{x+5}-3}{x-4}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
            
                </task>
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow 1} \frac{x^2 + 2x - 3}{x^2-1}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow 0} \frac{\sin(x) - x}{x^3}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
            
            </example>
            
            
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <p component="b" workspace="2in">
            Recall the definition of the derivative.
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            Recall the definition of the derivative. This is also an indeterminate limit, so maybe there's some hope that we can leverage this indeterminate limit into allowing us to evaluate other, more complicated ones.
        </p>

        <theorem xml:id="thm-Lhospital-rule">
            <title>L'Hospital's Rule</title>
            
            <statement>
                <p component="b" workspace="4in">
                    Let <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m> be differentiable functions so that 
                </p>
                <p component="a">
                    Let <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m> be differentiable functions so that <md>
                        \lim_{x\rightarrow a} f(x) = 0 \text{ and } \lim_{x\rightarrow a} g(x) = 0
                    </md> or 
                    <md>
                        \lim_{x\rightarrow a} f(x) = \pm \infty \text{ and } \lim_{x\rightarrow a} g(x) = \pm \infty
                    </md>.
                    Then
                    <md>
                        \lim_{x\rightarrow a} \frac{f(x)}{g(x)} = \lim_{x\rightarrow a} \frac{f'(x)}{g'(x)}
                    </md>, provided this limit exists or is <m>\pm \infty</m>.
                                         
                </p>
            </statement>
        </theorem>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow 1}\frac{x^2 + 2x - 3}{x^2 - 1}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

</handout>

<handout component="cur">
    <title>Wednesday 11/4</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Apply L'Hospital's Rule in a variety of scenarios.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Understand when L'Hospital's Rule does and does not apply.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>L'Hospital's Rule</title>

<p>
    Last time, we introduced L'Hospital's Rule as a method for computing specific indeterminate limits.
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-Lhospital-rule-two">
            <title>L'Hospital's Rule</title>
            
            <statement>
                <p component="b" workspace="4in">
                    Let <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m> be differentiable functions so that 
                </p>
                <p component="a">
                    Let <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m> be differentiable functions so that <md>
                        \lim_{x\rightarrow a} f(x) = 0 \text{ and } \lim_{x\rightarrow a} g(x) = 0
                    </md> or 
                    <md>
                        \lim_{x\rightarrow a} f(x) = \pm \infty \text{ and } \lim_{x\rightarrow a} g(x) = \pm \infty
                    </md>.
                    Then
                    <md>
                        \lim_{x\rightarrow a} \frac{f(x)}{g(x)} = \lim_{x\rightarrow a} \frac{f'(x)}{g'(x)}
                    </md>, provided this limit exists or is <m>\pm \infty</m>.
                                         
                </p>
            </statement>
        </theorem>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The Rayleigh-Jeans law for black-body radiation of wavelength <m>\lambda</m> is <md>
                        B_{RJ}(\lambda) = \frac{K}{\lambda^4}
                    </md>. Factoring in quantum mechanics gives Planck's law for radiation as
                 <md>
                    B_P(\lambda) = \frac{k_1}{\lambda^5} \frac{1}{e^{k_2/\lambda} - 1}
                 </md>. What happens to Planck's radiation as <m>\lambda \rightarrow \infty</m>? What about as <m>\lambda \rightarrow 0</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
    </page>

   <page>
        <example workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The <q>entropy</q> or uncertainty of a probabilistic experiment is computed as the sum over all outcomes of <m>-x \log(p(x))</m>, where <m>p(x)</m> is the probability of the outcome <m>x</m> happening. What is the uncertainty if <m>p(x) = 1</m>? What about if it is zero? Can we see this from the entropy?
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
        
    </page>

   <page>
        <p component="b" workspace="3in">
            There are some important remarks on using L'Hospital's Rule:
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            There are some important remarks on using L'Hospital's Rule: It's not the quotient rule. Make sure the conditions apply. It's also valid for one-sided limits and limits where the input goes to infinity.
        </p>
    </page>

<page>
    <example workspace="2in">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Evaluate each of the following.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow 1} \frac{\ln(x)}{x-1}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
    
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow 4} \frac{x^2 + 3x - 1}{x+2}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
    
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow 0} \frac{\sin(5x)}{\tan(4x)}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
    
        </task>
    
    </example>
    
</page>

<page>
    <example workspace="4in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Compute the following limit using L'Hopital's Rule.
                <md>
                    \lim_{x\rightarrow 0} \frac{e^{3x} - \cos(7x)}{\sin(4x)}
                </md>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>
    
</page>

<page>
    <example workspace="4in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Compute the following limit using L'Hopital's Rule.
                <md>
                    \lim_{x\rightarrow 0} \frac{\sin(x) - x}{x^3}
                </md>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>
    
</page>

<page>
    <example workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Compute the following limit using L'Hopital's Rule.
                <md>
                    \lim_{x\rightarrow \infty} \frac{3x^2 - 2x + 4}{2x^2 - 5x + 9}
                </md>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>

    <example workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Compute the following limit using L'Hopital's Rule.
                <md>
                    \lim_{x\rightarrow \infty} \frac{e^x}{x^4}
                </md>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>
    
</page>

<page>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Other Indeterminate Forms</title>
        
<p component="b" workspace="3in">
    So far, we have seen <m>\frac{0}{0}</m> and <m>\frac{\infty}{\infty}</m> as indeterminate forms and how to use L'Hospital's rule to solve them. However, there are several other indeterminate forms:
</p>
<p component="a">
    So far, we have seen <m>\frac{0}{0}</m> and <m>\frac{\infty}{\infty}</m> as indeterminate forms and how to use L'Hospital's rule to solve them. However, there are several other indeterminate forms: Product, difference, powers. The trick is to convert it into a fraction and then apply the normal L'Hospital's rule. 
</p>

    </paragraphs>
</page>

<page>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Indeterminate Products</title>
        
        <p component="b" workspace="2in">
            An indeterminate product is one of the form <m>0 \cdot \infty</m>. What can we do here?
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            An indeterminate product is one of the form <m>0 \cdot \infty</m>. What can we do here? Write out with functions f and g. Rewrite as a fraction.
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

<example workspace="2in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Compute <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow 0^+} x \ln(x)</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>

</example>

</page>

<page>
    <example workspace="3in">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Compute each of the limits using L'Hospital's Rule.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow \frac{pi}{2}^-} \left(x^2 - \frac{\pi^2}{4}\right)\tan(x)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
    
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow 0^+} \sqrt{x}\ln(x)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    
    </example>
    
</page>

<page>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Indeterminate Differences</title>
        
<p component="b" workspace="2in">
    Indeterminate Differences are of the form <m>\infty - \infty</m>. 
</p>
<p component="a">
    Indeterminate Differences are of the form <m>\infty - \infty</m> What's the approach here? Make it a single fraction, then apply L'Hospital's Rule.
</p>

<example workspace="2in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Compute <md>
                \lim_{x\rightarrow 0} \frac{1}{x} - \frac{1}{\sin(x)}
            </md>.
            
        </p>
    </statement>

</example>


    </paragraphs>
</page>

<page>
    <example workspace="4in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Compute <md>
                    \lim_{x\rightarrow \infty} x - \sqrt{x^2 + 1}
                </md>.
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>
    
</page>

<page>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Indeterminate Powers</title>
        <p component="b" workspace="4in">
            There are several forms of indeterminate powers: <m>0^0</m>, <m>\infty^0</m>, <m>1^\infty</m>. How can we get the issue out of the exponent?
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            There are several forms of indeterminate powers: <m>0^0</m>, <m>\infty^0</m>, <m>1^\infty</m>. How can we get the issue out of the exponent? Take logs! Then it's a product and we know how to handle those. Make sure to undo the log at the end.
        </p>
    </paragraphs>
</page>

<page>
    <example workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Compute the limit 
                <md>
                    \lim_{x\rightarrow \infty} \left(1 + \frac{1}{x}\right)^x
                </md>.
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>

    </page>

<page>

    <example workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Compute the limit 
                <md>
                    \lim_{x\rightarrow 0^+} \left(1 + \sin(3x)\right)^{1/x}
                </md>.
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>
    
</page>
</handout>

<handout component="cur">
    <title>Friday 11/6</title>
    <objectives>
