                <p>
                    Apply the Mean Value Theorem to analyze functions.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>

    <page>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Indeterminate Differences</title>
        
<p component="b" workspace="2in">
    Indeterminate Differences are of the form <m>\infty - \infty</m>. 
</p>
<p component="a">
    Indeterminate Differences are of the form <m>\infty - \infty</m> What's the approach here? Make it a single fraction, then apply L'Hospital's Rule.
</p>

<example workspace="2in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Compute <md>
                \lim_{x\rightarrow 0} \frac{1}{x} - \frac{1}{\sin(x)}
            </md>.
            
        </p>
    </statement>

</example>


    </paragraphs>
</page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>The Mean Value Theorem</title>

<p component="b" workspace="4in">
    The next portion of this course is all about how we can use the derivative to analyze functions.
</p>
<p component="a">
    The next portion of this course is all about how we can use the derivative to analyze functions. These include finding max/min of functions, determining behavior, and drawing graphs. For today, we'll get a part of overall behavior and tie that in to everything else next week.
</p>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <p>
            The Mean Value Theorem allows us to discuss the general behavior of a function given information about the derivative. It also has a fairly intuitive statement in terms of velocity.
        </p>

        <theorem xml:id="thm-mvt">
            <title>The Mean Value Theorem</title>          
            <statement>
                <p component="b" workspace="3in">
                    Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function defined on an interval <m>[a,b]</m> so that
                </p>
                <p component="a">
                    Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function defined on an interval <m>[a,b]</m> so that f is continuous on the closed interval and differentiable on the open. Then secant tangent. Tie in to velocities. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </theorem>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Determine if the function <m>f(x) = x^3 - 2x</m> satifies the conditions of the Mean Value Theorem on the interval <m>[1,4]</m>. If so, find the value of <m>c</m> that is guaranteed to exist by this theorem. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Consquences of the Mean Value Theorem</title>

            <p>
                There are a lot of useful consequences of the Mean Value Theorem, since it allows us to tie the derivative to function values.
            </p>

            <corollary xml:id="cor-constant-deriv">
                <statement>
                    <p component="b" workspace="2in">
                        Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function that is continuous on <m>[a,b]</m> and differentiable on <m>(a,b)</m>. If <m>f'(x) = 0</m> on <m>(a,b)</m>, 
                    </p>
                    <p component="a">
                        Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function that is continuous on <m>[a,b]</m> and differentiable on <m>(a,b)</m>. If <m>f'(x) = 0</m> on <m>(a,b)</m>, then <m>f(x)</m> is constant on <m>[a,b]</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </corollary>

            <corollary xml:id="cor-same-deriv">
                <statement>
                    <p component="b" workspace="2in">
                        Let <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m> be a functions that are continuous on <m>[a,b]</m> and differentiable on <m>(a,b)</m>. If <m>f'(x) = g'(x)</m> on <m>[a,b]</m>, 
                    </p>
                    <p component="a">
                        Let <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m> be a functions that are continuous on <m>[a,b]</m> and differentiable on <m>(a,b)</m>. If <m>f'(x) = g'(x)</m> on <m>[a,b]</m>, then <m>f(x) = g(x) + C</m> for some constant <m>C</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </corollary>
            
        </paragraphs>
    </page>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Increasing and Decreasing Functions</title>
            
            <p>
                The other main application of the Mean Value Theorem is to analyzing increasing and decreasing functions.
            </p>

            <definition xml:id="def-inc-dec-fcns">
                <statement>
                    <p component="b" workspace="3in">
                        Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function defined on an interval <m>(a,b)</m>. We say that <m>f(x)</m> is
                    </p>
                    <p component="a">
                        Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function defined on an interval <m>(a,b)</m>. We say that <m>f(x)</m> is increasing, decreasing on <m>(a,b)</m>. This is really hard to check.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </definition>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <corollary xml:id="cor-inc-dec-mvt">
            <statement>
                    <p component="b" workspace="2in">
                        Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function that is continuous on <m>[a,b]</m> and differentiable on <m>(a,b)</m>. 
                        If <m>f'(x) \gt 0</m> on <m>(a,b)</m>, 
                    </p>
                    <p component="a">
                        Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function that is continuous on <m>[a,b]</m> and differentiable on <m>(a,b)</m>. If <m>f'(x) \gt 0</m> on <m>(a,b)</m>, then <m>f(x)</m> is increasing on <m>(a,b)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </corollary>
    </page>

</handout>

</section>